// Whole numbers, such as `42`. Unsigned integers (`IntU...`) hold 0 to
// 2^N - 1, and signed integers (`IntS...`) hold -2^(N-1) to 2^(N-1) - 1, where
// N is the number of bits, as in `IntU8`. `IntU` and `IntS` are as wide as a
// pointer on the computer the program is built for. Arithmetic that goes
// outside a type's range stops the program, and so does dividing by zero.
// Only signed integers can be negated, and only unsigned integers can be
// shifted, with `.<` and `.>`, by an `Int`, which stops the program if the
// amount isn't from 0 to N - 1, or if `.<` shifts off a `1`.
//
// Integers are equal if they hold the same value, and are ordered by their
// values. Their operators work on two of the same type, except that shifts
// are by an `Int`. Each integer type is in its own file, such as `IntU8` in
// `intu8` and `IntS` in `ints`.
//
// Each integer type's `new` converts from any other integer type, as in
// `IntS16.new(small)`. It gives the type itself if it can hold every value of
// the other type, or an `Option` of it that's `none` for values it can't
// hold, as in `IntS8.new(big)`. `IntS` and `IntU` are treated as being at
// least 16 bits, with no limit on how wide they can be, so which conversions
// give an `Option` is the same on every CPU, including ones with pointers
// wider than 64 bits. Programs can't be built for CPUs with narrower
// pointers.

// The integer to use unless another is needed. Integer literals are `Int`s
// unless they're used as another integer type. It's `IntS`, so its width
// depends on the CPU.
alias Int = IntS
